import{_ as n,q as r,o as s,c as o,b as a,k as i,j as h,m as c}from"./chunks/framework.D3-xQjZb.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"What Makes a Software Requirement Good Enough for an AI Agent?","description":"Turn a user’s request into an observable software requirement, a bounded task for a coding agent, and a test a teammate can run independently.","frontmatter":{"title":"What Makes a Software Requirement Good Enough for an AI Agent?","description":"Turn a user’s request into an observable software requirement, a bounded task for a coding agent, and a test a teammate can run independently.","teaser":"“The order went through” sounds precise until someone refreshes the page. One extra sentence can make a software requirement testable.","articleGenre":"Engineering essay"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"research/systems/english-software-engineering.md","filePath":"research/systems/english-software-engineering.md"}'),d={name:"research/systems/english-software-engineering.md"};function l(m,e,u,g,p,f){const t=r("ArticleHeader");return s(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=a("h1",{id:"english-as-a-higher-abstraction-in-software-engineering",tabindex:"-1"},[i("What Makes a Software Requirement Good Enough for an AI Agent? "),a("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#english-as-a-higher-abstraction-in-software-engineering","aria-label":'Permalink to "What Makes a Software Requirement Good Enough for an AI Agent? {#english-as-a-higher-abstraction-in-software-engineering}"'},"​")],-1)),h(t),e[1]||(e[1]=c('<p>Imagine a team building a campus storefront. A customer clicks <em>Buy</em> and sees a confirmation. Everyone can agree the demo looks right. Then someone closes the tab and asks the coordinator to find the order. That second person has exposed a requirement the first screen never had to meet.</p><p>I sometimes tell students that “English is the new computer science.” What I mean is narrower. With a coding agent at hand, an engineer can do consequential work by describing who needs something, under what conditions, and what another person should observe afterward. Ordinary words can carry that intent. The team still has to decide what those words mean in software.</p><h2 id="what-moves-upward" tabindex="-1">What moves upward <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-moves-upward" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What moves upward&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For the storefront, I would write the requirement in the customer’s language. <em>After I submit a request and close the page, the coordinator can reopen the app and find my request once.</em> Now the team can discuss whose account can see the record, what survives a restart, and what should happen if someone clicks twice.</p><p>In my courses, personas and journeys name the people involved. Themes and epics organize scope. A user story and its acceptance criteria belong in a versioned issue small enough for someone to try. A pull request links the change, the test, and the decision to accept it. A short specification saves the next person from reconstructing these decisions from several chats.</p><h2 id="testing-is-the-agent-harness" tabindex="-1">Testing is the agent harness <a class="header-anchor" href="#testing-is-the-agent-harness" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Testing is the agent harness&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The same approach applies below the interface. My MetaVis media prototype specifies reference images, deterministic sensor outputs, hardware checks, and performance budgets. One test checks whether rendering another frame allocates a new Metal texture after warm-up. A reviewer looking at the picture alone could miss that cost. <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/metavis-demo-projects/blob/main/Docs/TDD_STRATEGY.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">MetaVis test strategy</a> · <a href="https://github.com/kaw393939/metavis-demo-projects/blob/main/Tests/MetaVisSimulationTests/Perf/RenderAllocationTests.swift" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">GPU allocation test</a></p><p>Before I give a coding agent a task, I want an expected result that someone else can explain. If the agent changes the implementation and the expected answer together, a passing check tells the reviewer very little. I would ask for a separate decision on any change to the acceptance criteria.</p><h2 id="kanban-changes-when-agents-are-abundant" tabindex="-1">Kanban changes when agents are abundant <a class="header-anchor" href="#kanban-changes-when-agents-are-abundant" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Kanban changes when agents are abundant&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A small patch with one runnable example is easier to review than five simultaneous proposals for the same feature. Give the next reviewer what they need to act: what changed, how to exercise it, what remains uncertain, and which decision belongs to them. A reversible copy edit calls for a lighter process than a change to who can read customer records.</p><h2 id="what-research-is-needed" tabindex="-1">What research is needed <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-research-is-needed" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What research is needed&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A useful study would give teams matched tasks and compare an open-ended prompt, a conventional issue, and an agent task with explicit acceptance tests. I would measure defects, reviewer time, rework, recovery, and whether a student can explain a new case independently. Earlier studies of problem-solving stages and collaborative software work provide starting points for that design. <a href="https://digitalcommons.njit.edu/fac_pubs/16112/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Deek, Turoff, and McHugh</a> · <a href="https://digitalcommons.njit.edu/fac_pubs/19489/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DeFranco-Tommarello and Deek</a></p><p>Try it at a demo. Hand a teammate the keyboard and ask them to finish the sentence, <em>I’ll believe this works when I can …</em> The difference between their answer and yours is a requirement worth discussing.</p><p><a href="./">Research series</a> · <a href="./metavis-evidence-and-evaluation">MetaVis case study</a></p>',14))])}const k=n(d,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,k as default};
